1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
The human search for perfection is a quest born of the need

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:11,980
to return to the pure transcendent source,

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:17,920
to help us endure life and make it less mundane.

4
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:22,920
It can lead to obsession or to frustration.

5
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:28,060
But the striving for perfection often results in great beauty,

6
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:31,460
which reveals a joyous and spiritual purpose.

7
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,100
Religion is a celebration of faith.

8
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:48,740
The faithful come together in glorious buildings

9
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,580
which have great spiritual power.

10
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:08,060
They come under many names, temples, mosques, synagogues.

11
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:12,300
Over the last 1,000 years, the Gothic cathedral

12
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:16,640
has been a special awe-inspiring building,

13
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:20,240
heaven's perfection on earth.

14
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:23,460
Notre Dame in Paris, Chartres Cathedral

15
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:26,460
in the French countryside, the National Cathedral

16
00:01:26,461 --> 00:01:27,960
in Washington, DC.

17
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,860
Each is assembled from stone, held together by faith,

18
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:38,200
shrines for beauty and truth.

19
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:42,940
Tonight on Modern Marvels, The Gothic Cathedrals.

20
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:59,800
The dark images are part of our imagination,

21
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:02,300
towering, oppressive stone monoliths

22
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:06,940
where the pious, deformed, broken men find solace and shelter

23
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,540
among the grinning gargoyles.

24
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,860
But in reality, Gothic cathedrals

25
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:25,360
are celebrations of the spirit.

26
00:02:25,361 --> 00:02:28,360
They are structures of overwhelming strength,

27
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:31,360
designed to teach and inspire through the beauty

28
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:34,360
of their architecture and remarkable technical virtuosity.

29
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,900
Here at Notre Dame in Paris, the genius of Gothic beauty

30
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,820
is abundantly clear.

31
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:45,920
The Gothic architect wanted your eye

32
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:50,320
to ascend toward heaven, drawing your eye upward.

33
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:53,360
That beautiful arch draws your eye up as high

34
00:02:53,361 --> 00:02:56,020
up as high as it can possibly go.

35
00:02:56,021 --> 00:02:59,960
Because people in the Middle Ages really wanted

36
00:02:59,961 --> 00:03:04,640
to build these great castles of the spirit, if you will.

37
00:03:04,641 --> 00:03:07,660
And they believed that a cathedral should be a place

38
00:03:07,661 --> 00:03:11,680
of comfort, where you could come in from the outside,

39
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:14,880
leaving your cares, your worries, your sins behind,

40
00:03:14,881 --> 00:03:17,960
and enter into the presence of God.

41
00:03:17,961 --> 00:03:20,860
That's what they were trying to achieve in the Middle Ages,

42
00:03:20,861 --> 00:03:23,820
was a convergence of heaven on earth.

43
00:03:23,821 --> 00:03:27,620
They wanted to build these great cathedrals

44
00:03:27,621 --> 00:03:31,100
to the glory of God, to be, if you will,

45
00:03:31,101 --> 00:03:34,040
a city of God here on earth.

46
00:03:34,041 --> 00:03:37,240
And so there is, in a sense, a convergence.

47
00:03:37,241 --> 00:03:41,620
Man is imperfect, but we are created in God's image.

48
00:03:41,621 --> 00:03:47,160
And so we do our best to create these beautiful cathedrals

49
00:03:47,161 --> 00:03:51,660
to the glory of God in homage to him.

50
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:55,000
But a cathedral is more than an homage to God.

51
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:57,800
It serves a specific purpose.

52
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:02,600
Its name comes from the Greek word kathedra, which means seat.

53
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:08,040
And a cathedral is a church which houses the seat of a bishop.

54
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:10,280
As a center of church power, a cathedral

55
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:13,060
is also a center of learning and inspiration,

56
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:15,560
demanding an awesome architectural presence,

57
00:04:15,561 --> 00:04:16,720
which matches its importance.

58
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:22,240
In the 12th century, that was best expressed

59
00:04:22,241 --> 00:04:26,580
through an innovative new kind of design called Gothic.

60
00:04:26,581 --> 00:04:28,640
Originally, it was a style of architecture

61
00:04:28,641 --> 00:04:35,480
that in some minds was thought to be rather bizarre and unusual.

62
00:04:35,481 --> 00:04:39,320
Gothic has three basic elements, actually.

63
00:04:39,321 --> 00:04:41,660
Number one, rib vaulting.

64
00:04:41,661 --> 00:04:43,680
Number two, flying buttresses.

65
00:04:43,681 --> 00:04:47,280
and thirdly, and in some ways the most important, the pointed

66
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:49,380
arch.

67
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,880
The concept was a radical departure

68
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:53,680
from early Roman churches, which

69
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:56,700
were small castle-like structures with small windows

70
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:59,860
and massive walls.

71
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,760
Instead, a Gothic cathedral began

72
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:04,160
with an ambitious floor plan called a cruciform,

73
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:08,100
resembling the shape of the Christian cross.

74
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:11,800
Because great height would eventually rise from this design,

75
00:05:11,801 --> 00:05:14,940
Construction efforts began with a solid foundation.

76
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:18,780
Layers of stone blocks set 25 feet into the earth

77
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:21,380
to support the building's great weight.

78
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,980
Each block needed to be perfectly level and aligned

79
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:25,920
or the entire structure could collapse,

80
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:30,160
as was the case in Bouvre, France, where the pieces of a 1284

81
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:32,720
AD cathedral which fell during construction

82
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,860
are a haunting reminder of imperfect masonry.

83
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,300
Today, only a section of the building remains,

84
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,920
too heavy and cumbersome to be disassembled and rebuilt

85
00:05:40,921 --> 00:05:43,820
as intended.

86
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:46,660
It might appear that grand granite pillars

87
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:50,000
are providing a cathedral structural support,

88
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:52,700
but actually, it is the walls which hold the building up.

89
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:57,680
In earlier times, this would necessitate

90
00:05:57,780 --> 00:05:59,800
very thick and solid sides.

91
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:02,840
But the Gothic aesthetic demands an innovation never before

92
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:07,020
possible in giant structures, enormous expanses of glass

93
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:09,880
embedded in the walls to shower the inside

94
00:06:09,881 --> 00:06:12,820
with a holy light.

95
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:15,380
Cutting away sections of the stone walls, however,

96
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:18,220
reduced the overall stability of the building.

97
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:21,320
The solution was an ingenious 12th century notion

98
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:26,640
to support or buttress the construction.

99
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:30,540
Traditionally, a building's roof is supported by its walls.

100
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:32,840
In early structures, entrances into a building

101
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:36,540
evolved into arches and load-bearing columns.

102
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,680
But as wooden roofs were replaced

103
00:06:38,681 --> 00:06:42,920
stone ceilings called vaults, walls became thicker

104
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:48,420
to support the increased weight and resist the outward thrust.

105
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:50,920
In a refinement of the principle, the downward weight

106
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:53,320
of the ceiling was directed to specific points

107
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:54,920
over the columns, which were thickened

108
00:06:55,020 --> 00:06:57,340
for the additional load.

109
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:00,340
The result was a building which resembled a ribbed skeleton,

110
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:03,380
and it allowed the walls to be opened even farther

111
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:06,340
for larger windows.

112
00:07:06,341 --> 00:07:09,180
But as cathedrals grew larger and taller,

113
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:12,180
the structure's weight inside and the force of the wind

114
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:13,980
outside increased.

115
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:16,380
To counteract these two unstable forces

116
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,380
without thickening the walls and reducing

117
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,980
the size of the windows, additional support

118
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:25,100
was placed outside the interior space.

119
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:28,460
The result was a row of buttresses connected to the walls

120
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,940
by stone arms called flying buttresses.

121
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:34,400
Here, the downward thrust of the ceiling

122
00:07:34,401 --> 00:07:37,640
pushes against the wall, then travels across the expanse

123
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:40,000
of the flying buttresses and is channeled

124
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:42,340
through the outside buttress columns

125
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:46,140
to the foundation below.

126
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:49,740
Most buttresses are topped by an ornate pinnacle.

127
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:52,660
These festive stone designs are not simply to enhance

128
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:54,060
the beauty of the structure.

129
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,060
It is their weight which helps the buttresses

130
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:59,060
perform their task.

131
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:04,400
You ended up with very thin, very tall, very thin walls.

132
00:08:04,401 --> 00:08:08,640
which were made of stones piled up.

133
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:13,140
So the buttresses stopped the whole building from pushing out

134
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:15,840
and the vault falling.

135
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:18,880
A vault is the architectural name for the unique ceiling

136
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:20,980
construction of a Gothic cathedral

137
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:24,460
and part of the genius behind its design.

138
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:28,520
First, temporary wooden centerings are mounted on scaffolding.

139
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:31,720
Then, cut stones are laid in place from the bottom up

140
00:08:31,721 --> 00:08:35,420
up and mortared together, though it is their own downward weight

141
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:37,920
which keep them firmly set together.

142
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:40,620
Finally, a keystone completes the assembly,

143
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:43,760
so when the temporary centering are removed,

144
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:48,380
only the stone frame of the ceiling remains.

145
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:50,480
The expanse of the vaulting within the frame

146
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:52,980
begins with a webbing of very lightweight stone

147
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:56,520
mortared together with a layer of wood planking underneath.

148
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:59,720
Once the mortar is set, a four-inch layer of concrete

149
00:08:59,721 --> 00:09:02,720
is poured over the entire area to stabilize the structure.

150
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:09,120
The process is simply repeated until the entire ceiling

151
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:11,060
area has been completed.

152
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:13,200
And because the building and its ceiling

153
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:15,140
are primarily made of stone, there

154
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:18,200
is little chance of fire damage, a primary concern

155
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:23,040
in an age when most secular buildings were made of wood.

156
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:26,420
I think you can say that it is an act of faith.

157
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:28,780
It has to do with gravity.

158
00:09:28,781 --> 00:09:32,420
It has to do with the thrust of weight.

159
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:34,680
When you build a Gothic cathedral,

160
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:38,420
you start at the bottom, laying stone upon stone,

161
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:42,660
course upon course, and then you pray.

162
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:46,360
But as I say, it works.

163
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:49,600
Using such heavy and ponderous materials in constructions,

164
00:09:49,700 --> 00:09:51,840
it seems a miracle that Gothic cathedrals

165
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:54,180
would be homes of holy light.

166
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:57,740
But that was their central and original intent.

167
00:09:57,741 --> 00:10:01,080
We sort of glom on to a date for the beginning of Gothic.

168
00:10:01,081 --> 00:10:03,780
We think of the Abbot Suger in France,

169
00:10:03,781 --> 00:10:06,740
who redid his abbey in 1130.

170
00:10:06,741 --> 00:10:10,180
And he, in many ways, is known as the father of stained glass

171
00:10:10,181 --> 00:10:12,280
because he believed that stained glass

172
00:10:12,281 --> 00:10:14,080
let in divine light.

173
00:10:14,081 --> 00:10:17,000
Natural light coming through colored glass,

174
00:10:17,001 --> 00:10:21,400
in a sense, was divine and created a sort of a mystery.

175
00:10:21,401 --> 00:10:25,100
And so the stained glass windows were very, very important.

176
00:10:25,101 --> 00:10:29,580
And coming to a Gothic cathedral is really a visible experience.

177
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:31,080
It's visual.

178
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:34,180
You see, and it is through your sight

179
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:38,380
that you are able to interpret often the meaning of your faith.

180
00:10:48,360 --> 00:10:51,400
Religion acts on faith, and sometimes faith

181
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:54,000
is bolstered by relics, objects believed

182
00:10:54,001 --> 00:10:57,340
to have been handled by saints or even decaying pieces

183
00:10:57,440 --> 00:10:58,640
of the saints themselves.

184
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:03,840
Most of these relics have long since perished,

185
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:06,020
and there is great debate as to whether these relics

186
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:08,840
ever existed at all.

187
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:12,580
At Chartres Cathedral, 60 miles south of the modern city of Paris,

188
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:14,920
one of the world's most important Gothic structures

189
00:11:15,020 --> 00:11:19,720
was built to house such a relic, the shawl of the Virgin Mary.

190
00:11:19,721 --> 00:11:20,421
Mary.

191
00:11:20,422 --> 00:11:27,400
So it's here that Mary, the queen of all the saints, would intercede for humanity

192
00:11:27,401 --> 00:11:31,322
most favorably because this is where her sacred relic is kept.

193
00:11:31,323 --> 00:11:33,220
That explains the size of this

194
00:11:33,221 --> 00:11:37,577
great building. On a clear day like this can be seen for 30, 40 miles.

195
00:11:37,578 --> 00:11:38,760
Our modern visitors

196
00:11:38,761 --> 00:11:43,075
drive or come in buses or trains. The medieval pilgrim walked here.

197
00:11:43,076 --> 00:11:44,620
Imagine you could see it

198
00:11:44,621 --> 00:11:48,060
then for three or four days getting closer and closer.

199
00:11:48,061 --> 00:11:51,460
1194, the great fire destroyed much of the city

200
00:11:51,461 --> 00:11:53,620
and the preceding 11th century church,

201
00:11:53,621 --> 00:11:57,800
which stood behind me, was badly damaged.

202
00:11:57,801 --> 00:12:00,020
At first it was feared that the holy relic

203
00:12:00,021 --> 00:12:04,320
given to shout by King Charles the Bald in 876

204
00:12:04,321 --> 00:12:06,000
had burnt in the fire.

205
00:12:06,001 --> 00:12:07,640
But three days after the fire,

206
00:12:07,641 --> 00:12:10,920
we're told that it was brought out to the crypts unharmed.

207
00:12:10,921 --> 00:12:14,720
And this was proclaimed a miraculous sign from Mary

208
00:12:14,721 --> 00:12:17,600
that she desired a more praiseworthy church.

209
00:12:17,601 --> 00:12:18,860
And she got it.

210
00:12:18,861 --> 00:12:21,960
We do not know who built Shark Cathedral.

211
00:12:21,961 --> 00:12:23,860
I know this is strange.

212
00:12:23,861 --> 00:12:25,600
We know who built the pyramids or the Parthenon,

213
00:12:25,601 --> 00:12:28,180
but not 13th century Shark Cathedral,

214
00:12:28,181 --> 00:12:30,300
or how many master masons were involved,

215
00:12:30,301 --> 00:12:33,180
or who the craftsmen were, who sculpted John the Baptist

216
00:12:33,181 --> 00:12:36,040
in the north door behind me, or God creating Adam.

217
00:12:36,041 --> 00:12:38,920
So they didn't sign their work.

218
00:12:38,921 --> 00:12:41,160
What is known about the construction of Chartres

219
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:44,200
are the people who helped fund the effort.

220
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:47,320
The giant stained glass windows acknowledge local merchants

221
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:49,260
who sponsored the art.

222
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:51,360
Instead of their names, they are identified

223
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,640
by images of themselves performing the crafts

224
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:55,800
which raised the money.

225
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:02,140
Like all Gothic cathedrals, the exterior design of Chartres

226
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:05,080
is meant to be an illustrated history for the faithful who

227
00:13:05,180 --> 00:13:08,040
were unable to decipher the written word.

228
00:13:08,041 --> 00:13:10,340
Instead, three-dimensional figures depict

229
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:14,480
great theological notions.

230
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:17,080
Thousands of details, tens of thousands,

231
00:13:17,180 --> 00:13:19,480
in fact, in the sculpture on the outside.

232
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:22,980
In fact, we can read Chartres Cathedral like a book.

233
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:23,980
It's a long story.

234
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:25,700
The beginning is behind me.

235
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:29,900
The creation cycle, the story of Adam and Eve

236
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and paradise was lost.

237
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:34,840
In the outer archivalts of the central bay of the North Door,

238
00:13:34,940 --> 00:13:37,800
where we're standing, two archivalts,

239
00:13:37,801 --> 00:13:43,100
On the outside, God creates, and on the inside, the things that are being created.

240
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:48,700
So if you start at the bottom, God created, and there are inscriptions in Latin,

241
00:13:49,260 --> 00:13:52,920
created the heaven and the earth, meaning God created all things.

242
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:55,300
The first day is the second up.

243
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:58,120
God created the day and night.

244
00:13:58,380 --> 00:13:59,860
Night is blindfolded.

245
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:02,320
Day leads night by the hand.

246
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:03,600
Note that personified.

247
00:14:03,601 --> 00:14:09,600
Next, God separated the firmaments and created fire, earth, water and air.

248
00:14:09,601 --> 00:14:16,600
Next, he created the plants, then the sun, the moon and the planets, then the birds and the fish.

249
00:14:16,601 --> 00:14:20,600
And when you come to Shart, I should add, including pigeons.

250
00:14:20,601 --> 00:14:24,600
Next, God created the animals.

251
00:14:24,601 --> 00:14:26,600
You might see a lion.

252
00:14:26,601 --> 00:14:27,600
Then some more trees.

253
00:14:27,601 --> 00:14:31,600
The Garden of Eden, sometimes referred to as Salem.

254
00:14:31,601 --> 00:14:33,900
American audiences will know that word.

255
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,980
Many American towns are named Salem, Jerusalem, Salem, Shalom.

256
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:41,140
The word means peace.

257
00:14:41,260 --> 00:14:43,040
So the Garden of Eden was a place of peace.

258
00:14:43,540 --> 00:14:46,940
Then a very remarkable detail, difficult to find when you come to show up,

259
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:50,560
but the very top on the outside, God creating Adam.

260
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:53,480
Adam is kneeling with his head on God's knee,

261
00:14:53,481 --> 00:14:55,860
and God is caressing Adam's head gently,

262
00:14:55,980 --> 00:14:59,380
always like a divine potter fashioning Adam's head out of clay.

263
00:14:59,381 --> 00:15:01,880
It is a mistake to consider the iconography

264
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:05,280
of a Gothic cathedral as intimidating and moralistic

265
00:15:05,380 --> 00:15:06,780
storytelling.

266
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:09,720
There is a lightness and joy to the figures,

267
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:13,920
which has been lost to the ages.

268
00:15:14,020 --> 00:15:15,900
Originally, these exterior sculptures

269
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,040
were hand-painted in bright luminous colors.

270
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:21,140
Over the years, the paint has worn away,

271
00:15:21,240 --> 00:15:24,400
and later generations have become more accustomed to the gray

272
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:27,440
figures we see today.

273
00:15:27,441 --> 00:15:30,940
Perhaps the most striking features of Gothic cathedrals

274
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:34,140
are the architectural devices used to divert rainwater

275
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:36,680
from the roof away from the sides of the building.

276
00:15:36,780 --> 00:15:39,880
They are called gargoyles, and their hollow form

277
00:15:39,980 --> 00:15:43,060
is a funnel which allows water to spill from their mouths

278
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:46,720
with a distinctive gargling sound.

279
00:15:46,820 --> 00:15:49,300
A gargoyle, strictly speaking, comes

280
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,300
from the word gargoy in French.

281
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:55,300
Gargoyer is given as an English gargle and gargle, water,

282
00:15:55,301 --> 00:15:58,400
You see, so a gargoyle is a water spout.

283
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:01,000
You can see others around the building.

284
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:05,040
Many Gothic cathedrals are also festooned with grotesques,

285
00:16:05,140 --> 00:16:08,020
equally hideous creatures who both frighten evil spirits

286
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:10,920
away from the sanctuary and peer longingly

287
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:14,280
at the pious congregations below.

288
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:17,260
Grotesques serve no practical function,

289
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:20,000
yet they exert a powerful hold on the imagination

290
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:22,200
of most people who visit Gothic cathedrals.

291
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:30,540
Their exaggerated expressions mirror our ecstatic love of God.

292
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:33,900
Their sad countenance speaks to our sense of humility

293
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,840
before a higher power.

294
00:16:35,940 --> 00:16:38,540
Their melancholy postures remind us

295
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:41,740
of how far we have strayed from the garden of paradise.

296
00:16:55,300 --> 00:16:58,740
It is the sixth largest cathedral in the world,

297
00:16:58,840 --> 00:17:03,440
surpassing the size of Chartres and Notre Dame in Paris.

298
00:17:03,540 --> 00:17:06,240
But unlike classic Gothic structures,

299
00:17:06,340 --> 00:17:08,540
the National Cathedral in Washington, DC,

300
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:14,120
was born of the passions of laymen and not of the clergy.

301
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:18,720
In 1891, Charles Glover Gathers, a prominent Washington banker,

302
00:17:18,820 --> 00:17:21,200
promoted the idea of a monumental cathedral

303
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:24,860
as a moral center within the nation's capital.

304
00:17:24,861 --> 00:17:27,960
Two years later, Congress granted a charter to the Protestant

305
00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:30,760
Episcopal Cathedral Foundation, and the search

306
00:17:30,860 --> 00:17:32,460
was on for a member of the clergy

307
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:36,240
to lead the construction effort.

308
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:38,500
The right Reverend Henry Yates Satterley

309
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:42,140
was elected the cathedral's first bishop in 1896,

310
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:45,520
without a building or a congregation to lead.

311
00:17:45,620 --> 00:17:49,020
But Satterley had a vision which would propel the project

312
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:51,660
for the next century.

313
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:54,320
He began by choosing a site for the building which would

314
00:17:54,321 --> 00:17:57,060
the best view of the city.

315
00:17:57,160 --> 00:17:59,460
Back when this site was selected, this was all woods.

316
00:17:59,560 --> 00:18:02,120
And this was, I guess, what one called the boondocks.

317
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:03,760
This was out of town.

318
00:18:03,860 --> 00:18:05,460
Generally speaking, this is considered

319
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:07,960
to be the highest site in Washington.

320
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:11,540
That's why he selected that, to be seen from downtown.

321
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:13,680
Satterly literally took the high ground,

322
00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:16,380
and with it, a philosophy for a cathedral which

323
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:18,420
would be facilitated by Episcopalians,

324
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:22,480
yet transcend all barriers of denomination.

325
00:18:22,481 --> 00:18:27,480
This would be an American church embracing all faiths.

326
00:18:27,580 --> 00:18:31,220
The design would come from George Frederick Bosley,

327
00:18:31,320 --> 00:18:34,460
the greatest Victorian Gothicist of his time,

328
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:37,160
who understood the enduring emotional impact

329
00:18:37,260 --> 00:18:40,860
that Gothic architecture bestows.

330
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:44,740
September 29, 1907, President Teddy Roosevelt

331
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:47,180
participates in the laying of the cornerstone,

332
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:50,080
composed of granite and smaller rocks

333
00:18:50,081 --> 00:18:53,680
harvested by pilgrims in the River Jordan.

334
00:18:53,780 --> 00:18:57,020
Over 10,000 people gathered to watch the ceremony.

335
00:18:57,120 --> 00:18:59,720
So many people, in fact, that local trolley lines

336
00:18:59,820 --> 00:19:01,820
broke under the strain.

337
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:04,560
Important members of Congress and the Supreme Court

338
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:07,060
stood by the Episcopalian House of Bishops

339
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:09,660
and watched from grandstand seats.

340
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:12,940
Their support was more spiritual than functional.

341
00:19:13,040 --> 00:19:17,200
From the very outset, this cathedral never has received

342
00:19:17,300 --> 00:19:19,200
any funds from the federal government.

343
00:19:19,201 --> 00:19:25,200
government and we have not ever received any church money because the National Episcopal

344
00:19:25,201 --> 00:19:26,460
Church is not supported.

345
00:19:26,461 --> 00:19:33,380
So this cathedral is an independent entity, an independent unity and money has been raised

346
00:19:33,381 --> 00:19:35,420
that way over the years.

347
00:19:35,421 --> 00:19:40,680
At any one time, the number of people involved in this cathedral are not what you would expect.

348
00:19:40,681 --> 00:19:42,100
It's a small number.

349
00:19:42,101 --> 00:19:47,800
The average crew was around 30 to 35, 40 men,

350
00:19:47,801 --> 00:19:51,640
masons, laborers, carpenters, and helpers.

351
00:19:51,641 --> 00:19:54,940
And of course, we had the architects and their team.

352
00:19:54,941 --> 00:19:58,480
We had the stone carvers.

353
00:19:58,481 --> 00:20:02,620
And then there's the stained glass window makers,

354
00:20:02,621 --> 00:20:04,020
the wrought iron workers and all.

355
00:20:12,100 --> 00:20:14,800
The mechanics of building a Gothic cathedral

356
00:20:14,900 --> 00:20:18,100
have not changed in over 900 years.

357
00:20:21,340 --> 00:20:24,240
Techniques used during the later stages of the work

358
00:20:24,340 --> 00:20:28,680
in the 1980s mirrors the way it was performed in 1907

359
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:32,060
and, of course, in the 12th century.

360
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:35,460
We still are laying the stones in a full bed of mortar

361
00:20:35,560 --> 00:20:38,320
using pretty much the traditional tools, the trowels,

362
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:41,160
the hammers, the wood, and the wood.

363
00:20:41,161 --> 00:20:44,900
the hammers, the mallets, that part hasn't changed.

364
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:46,700
Of course, the tools that we use,

365
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:50,280
such as electric hammers and electric drills

366
00:20:50,281 --> 00:20:52,140
and air compressors and things like that

367
00:20:52,141 --> 00:20:54,320
make our life much easier.

368
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:57,320
But we're still basically accomplishing the same things

369
00:20:57,321 --> 00:21:00,800
with those tools that the ancient stonemasons were doing.

370
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:04,360
The stones were then hoisted up to the towers

371
00:21:04,361 --> 00:21:06,680
by a tower crane that we had up there.

372
00:21:06,980 --> 00:21:09,460
From there, they were landed on the wall.

373
00:21:09,461 --> 00:21:12,300
and once they were landed on the wall,

374
00:21:12,301 --> 00:21:14,960
we proceeded to pick the stones up

375
00:21:14,961 --> 00:21:16,400
with our own lifting devices

376
00:21:16,401 --> 00:21:20,300
that we had built on top of our work area.

377
00:21:20,460 --> 00:21:22,380
We used pulleys and chain hoists.

378
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:25,200
The stones are laid on a full bed of mortar

379
00:21:25,201 --> 00:21:29,660
and each stone is individually beat into place.

380
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:32,580
We use the word beating the stone into place

381
00:21:32,581 --> 00:21:35,100
using large rawhide-tipped hammers.

382
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:38,860
There is no steel skeleton that's wrapped in stone.

383
00:21:38,861 --> 00:21:43,760
The stone that you see on the cathedral is the structure.

384
00:21:43,860 --> 00:21:47,900
One cubic foot of limestone weighs nearly 150 pounds.

385
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,800
The combined weight of all the limestone used here

386
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:54,400
exceeds 300 million pounds.

387
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:56,520
But limestone is a porous material.

388
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:58,620
And like classic European cathedrals,

389
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:02,220
gargoyles are a key structural element to protect the rock

390
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:04,220
from moisture damage.

391
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:06,720
Our cathedral is made of limestone.

392
00:22:06,721 --> 00:22:11,060
And so it's a porous stone, and it will retain moisture.

393
00:22:11,160 --> 00:22:14,900
So the gargoyles and grotesque prevent this moisture

394
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,200
from seeping into the walls.

395
00:22:17,300 --> 00:22:20,540
Perhaps the most dynamic Gothic influence on the cathedral

396
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:23,640
can be seen in the stained glass windows,

397
00:22:23,740 --> 00:22:25,900
more than 100 of them.

398
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,780
And there was a point at which God said, let there be light.

399
00:22:29,880 --> 00:22:35,580
And this window tells that story in a very modern, abstract way,

400
00:22:35,581 --> 00:22:41,520
with pieces of stained glass made not just a flat glass, by the way, but made a faceted glass,

401
00:22:41,980 --> 00:22:47,960
picking up the light from many different angles so that all times of the day, in all climates,

402
00:22:47,961 --> 00:22:53,160
all types of weather, you have this beautiful jewel-like quality of that window,

403
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:56,320
which reflects itself down the entire nave of the cathedral.

404
00:22:57,240 --> 00:23:01,400
A window is made first from the inspiration of the artist.

405
00:23:01,401 --> 00:23:06,600
then he actually prepares the window in panels and often is assisted by a

406
00:23:06,601 --> 00:23:12,980
fabricator who is an expert on doing just that. The execution of the window takes

407
00:23:12,981 --> 00:23:17,900
quite a while but the actual installation can be done in a short

408
00:23:17,901 --> 00:23:23,960
period of time. We want our pilgrims, if you will, to know that this cathedral

409
00:23:23,961 --> 00:23:29,120
represents the times in which you and I are living today, the 20th century, and a

410
00:23:29,121 --> 00:23:32,920
The window which does this very well is the space window.

411
00:23:33,020 --> 00:23:36,520
In that window is a very special piece of rock

412
00:23:36,620 --> 00:23:38,960
brought from the surface of the moon.

413
00:23:39,060 --> 00:23:41,360
So that in itself is a reminder.

414
00:23:41,460 --> 00:23:46,740
In that window, it says, is not God in the height of heaven?

415
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:49,340
Those words were spoken originally by Job in the Bible

416
00:23:49,440 --> 00:23:54,540
and repeated by Neil Armstrong as he orbited the moon.

417
00:23:54,640 --> 00:23:56,520
The bell tower is officially known

418
00:23:56,521 --> 00:23:59,660
known as the Gloria in Excelsis Tower.

419
00:23:59,661 --> 00:24:03,060
It rises in the sky 301 feet.

420
00:24:03,061 --> 00:24:05,960
It has two sets of bells in it.

421
00:24:05,961 --> 00:24:10,160
It has a 53-bell carillon, which is played by a carillon or.

422
00:24:10,161 --> 00:24:24,161
And above that is a bell ringing chamber, which holds 10 bells.

423
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:34,480
And these are actually pulled by ropes by bell ringers who are trained in this art.

424
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:37,780
We ring the bells regularly here at the cathedral.

425
00:24:37,781 --> 00:24:42,180
Bell ringing is an ancient art, both in terms of melody

426
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:44,580
and manufacture of the individual bells,

427
00:24:44,680 --> 00:24:50,520
which range in weight from 15 pounds to several tons.

428
00:24:50,620 --> 00:24:54,540
Bell makers begin with a clay model of the proposed shape,

429
00:24:54,640 --> 00:24:56,940
then cover it with a thick layer of wax

430
00:24:57,040 --> 00:25:00,140
whose depth matches the dimensions of the finished bell.

431
00:25:00,240 --> 00:25:03,140
The mold is covered with additional layers of clay and

432
00:25:03,240 --> 00:25:05,680
plaster, then heated over a fire.

433
00:25:05,681 --> 00:25:09,480
The wax runs out and leaves a cavity into which the molten

434
00:25:09,580 --> 00:25:11,580
bronze is poured.

435
00:25:11,680 --> 00:25:14,280
When the metal cools, the mold is broken

436
00:25:14,380 --> 00:25:18,120
and the bell is smoothed and tuned.

437
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:20,820
It is important that an extended cooling process

438
00:25:20,920 --> 00:25:24,140
be employed to ensure a perfect tone.

439
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:29,140
Then an expert bell master must supervise the installation.

440
00:25:29,240 --> 00:25:32,740
Achieving the exact rhythm between 10 separate musicians,

441
00:25:32,741 --> 00:25:36,340
each pulling a bell cord requires exhaustive practice.

442
00:25:36,440 --> 00:25:39,140
Their choreography is called changes

443
00:25:39,240 --> 00:25:41,540
and is often performed with stays,

444
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:43,880
holding still the bell clappers in order

445
00:25:43,980 --> 00:25:48,080
to avoid the deafening ringing during lengthy rehearsals.

446
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:52,400
When the stays are removed and the bells are allowed to sing,

447
00:25:52,500 --> 00:25:56,840
their glorious peal seems to announce a new hope.

448
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:59,400
This is the sound of a Gothic cathedral,

449
00:25:59,401 --> 00:26:03,100
as strong as the stone which defines its form

450
00:26:03,200 --> 00:26:05,800
and the people who define its spirit.

451
00:26:16,320 --> 00:26:19,620
The secret to carving stone is to imagine the beautiful figure

452
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:23,420
trapped in the rock and have the compassion to set it free.

453
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:29,060
Over the last four decades, Vincent Palumbo

454
00:26:29,061 --> 00:26:32,060
has dedicated his artistic compassion

455
00:26:32,160 --> 00:26:35,900
to the glory of the National Cathedral.

456
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
Tricks of the trade.

457
00:26:38,100 --> 00:26:43,500
We call it in Italian, we call it .

458
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:45,820
So that's how we build the cathedral,

459
00:26:45,920 --> 00:26:50,020
chiseling away little by little.

460
00:26:50,120 --> 00:26:55,020
All of a sudden, you see, wow, we did that.

461
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:56,320
Wow, we did that.

462
00:26:59,060 --> 00:27:08,560
We have 320 of those little angels that go around the two towers and everyone

463
00:27:08,561 --> 00:27:14,960
play music. They have a different instrument, musical instrument. This guy

464
00:27:14,961 --> 00:27:20,260
sounded tambourine, you know, it must be either Italian or Spanish, you know, with

465
00:27:20,261 --> 00:27:27,140
tambourine. But every other one, you've got the balalaika, trombone, trombone,

466
00:27:27,141 --> 00:27:30,540
Everything, everything with a musical instrument.

467
00:27:30,640 --> 00:27:34,580
Everyone is a musical instrument.

468
00:27:34,680 --> 00:27:37,420
Somewhere under the din of steel and stone,

469
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:40,860
there is a music Vincent hears, an inspiration,

470
00:27:40,960 --> 00:27:43,720
a revelation, a lyrical prayer, which

471
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:46,420
is articulated through the rhythmic process

472
00:27:46,520 --> 00:27:49,100
of his hand-wrought art.

473
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:50,960
I got a tool, see?

474
00:27:51,060 --> 00:27:53,400
We have tools smaller like this.

475
00:27:53,500 --> 00:27:54,640
Look, look at this.

476
00:27:54,641 --> 00:27:58,640
This, my father used these tools.

477
00:27:58,641 --> 00:28:02,640
This specific chisel, my father used this.

478
00:28:02,641 --> 00:28:06,640
I have tools that my grandfather used.

479
00:28:06,641 --> 00:28:10,640
So they're traditional. But you need hundreds of tools

480
00:28:10,641 --> 00:28:14,640
besides you because you're doing something all of a sudden,

481
00:28:14,641 --> 00:28:18,640
you need that shape. Look around and use that.

482
00:28:18,641 --> 00:28:22,640
My father died while working at the cathedral.

483
00:28:22,641 --> 00:28:26,140
not for accident, for natural cause,

484
00:28:26,141 --> 00:28:28,580
but he was working here when he died.

485
00:28:28,581 --> 00:28:33,520
So that's one of the reasons I stay so long here

486
00:28:33,521 --> 00:28:36,720
at the cathedral, you know, because it's sentimental reason.

487
00:28:36,721 --> 00:28:41,160
In so many years, in 34 years, I have other requests,

488
00:28:41,161 --> 00:28:45,060
you know, but I was not interested in my life

489
00:28:45,061 --> 00:28:47,240
has been here at the cathedral.

490
00:28:47,241 --> 00:28:50,960
Now you're looking at it, I'm the only one left, you know.

491
00:28:50,961 --> 00:28:54,400
Carving needed to be done, because that's the way

492
00:28:54,500 --> 00:28:56,300
we're going to complete the cathedral.

493
00:28:56,400 --> 00:29:00,200
The cathedral is finished, but it's not complete.

494
00:29:00,300 --> 00:29:07,280
We've got another maybe 60 or 70 years of carving to do, at least.

495
00:29:07,380 --> 00:29:09,720
To the casual observer, a Gothic cathedral

496
00:29:09,820 --> 00:29:12,520
appears to be built of stone, adorned with iron,

497
00:29:12,620 --> 00:29:16,720
and punctuated by the hard crystal colors of stained glass.

498
00:29:16,820 --> 00:29:20,060
But the sculpted textures of Vincent Palumbo

499
00:29:20,061 --> 00:29:23,160
embedded in its form, suggest a structure assembled

500
00:29:23,260 --> 00:29:27,100
from stronger materials, the spirit of the human's

501
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:31,340
passionate longing to celebrate the glory of God.

502
00:29:31,440 --> 00:29:36,140
You work so long, you feel you are a part of the building.

503
00:29:36,240 --> 00:29:37,680
That's how it is.

504
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:39,520
It's a beautiful building.

505
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:41,080
Everyone work of it.

506
00:29:41,180 --> 00:29:43,180
We are proud to be part of it.

507
00:29:43,280 --> 00:29:47,520
We can tell our grandchildren, you know,

508
00:29:47,521 --> 00:29:49,120
That's what your grandfather did.

509
00:29:59,260 --> 00:30:01,960
Cathedrals are like glorious machines which drive

510
00:30:02,060 --> 00:30:03,860
the mechanics of the faithful.

511
00:30:03,960 --> 00:30:06,140
Their magnificent height and holy light

512
00:30:06,240 --> 00:30:08,840
are more than expressions of awe.

513
00:30:08,940 --> 00:30:11,380
The structure's exuberant spirit reinforces the sense

514
00:30:11,480 --> 00:30:14,540
of holiness within ourselves.

515
00:30:14,640 --> 00:30:17,120
That is its function.

516
00:30:17,121 --> 00:30:18,660
The performance test of a cathedral

517
00:30:18,760 --> 00:30:21,420
is its resonance in the community.

518
00:30:21,520 --> 00:30:24,760
The National Cathedral is a living legacy of profound impact

519
00:30:24,860 --> 00:30:30,540
upon the character of Washington, DC, and the nation.

520
00:30:30,640 --> 00:30:32,900
Although facilitated by Episcopalians,

521
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:36,300
the Gothic structure has also been a temporary house of prayer

522
00:30:36,400 --> 00:30:40,040
for two synagogues and hosts services

523
00:30:40,140 --> 00:30:44,340
for the Greek Orthodox Church, Baptists, Roman Catholics,

524
00:30:44,440 --> 00:30:47,120
and Charismatic.

525
00:30:47,121 --> 00:30:53,300
Our services include preachers from just about every denomination within Christianity,

526
00:30:53,660 --> 00:30:55,980
as well as Jewish and Islamic speakers.

527
00:30:56,360 --> 00:31:02,540
So its vision and its purpose is to be a great house of prayer for all people.

528
00:31:03,060 --> 00:31:06,500
There are seven great faiths that come together at this cathedral

529
00:31:06,501 --> 00:31:13,300
to talk about how they can work together to heal the social ills in our society

530
00:31:13,301 --> 00:31:16,620
and to stand against religious bigotry.

531
00:31:17,120 --> 00:31:19,720
More than 1,500 religious services

532
00:31:19,820 --> 00:31:21,860
are performed here every year.

533
00:31:21,960 --> 00:31:24,720
2,000 people a day, more than a half million annually,

534
00:31:24,820 --> 00:31:26,660
visit the cathedral.

535
00:31:26,760 --> 00:31:29,860
Here, the faithful from every level of society

536
00:31:29,960 --> 00:31:30,860
celebrate together.

537
00:31:33,500 --> 00:31:37,080
It's true that we do attract a great number

538
00:31:37,180 --> 00:31:41,480
of international leaders, both political as well as religious

539
00:31:41,580 --> 00:31:42,880
leaders.

540
00:31:42,980 --> 00:31:46,240
I believe that they are drawn to this cathedral

541
00:31:46,241 --> 00:31:51,240
because it is a sign to them of the American religious experience.

542
00:31:51,340 --> 00:31:58,280
I think the cathedral is that kind of place

543
00:31:58,380 --> 00:32:02,860
that represents a national moral conscience.

544
00:32:02,960 --> 00:32:05,300
When musicians against nuclear arms

545
00:32:05,400 --> 00:32:08,160
needed to speak out against growing atomic stockpiles

546
00:32:08,260 --> 00:32:11,840
in the 1970s, it was here they sponsored a performance

547
00:32:11,940 --> 00:32:14,360
by Leonard Bernstein, which focused public attention

548
00:32:14,361 --> 00:32:15,600
attention on the situation.

549
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:22,060
When America mourned the tragic loss of life in Vietnam,

550
00:32:22,160 --> 00:32:26,240
it was here the nation came together to grieve.

551
00:32:26,340 --> 00:32:32,580
For 10 years, this nation struggled in Southeast Asia.

552
00:32:32,680 --> 00:32:37,080
2,700,000 American men and women participated

553
00:32:37,180 --> 00:32:40,820
in the armed forces in that struggle,

554
00:32:40,821 --> 00:32:47,660
And 57,939 did not return.

555
00:32:47,760 --> 00:32:50,960
It was a day and night service in which the names

556
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:55,460
of the Vietnam War dead were read aloud.

557
00:32:55,560 --> 00:32:57,540
Paul B. Abbott.

558
00:32:57,640 --> 00:32:59,700
Raymond L. Abbott.

559
00:32:59,800 --> 00:33:02,240
Robert E. Abbott, Jr.

560
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:04,480
Robert W. Abbott.

561
00:33:04,580 --> 00:33:06,720
Stephen G. Abbott.

562
00:33:06,820 --> 00:33:08,980
And when America's struggle for civil rights

563
00:33:08,981 --> 00:33:11,880
found a national hero in Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr.

564
00:33:11,980 --> 00:33:14,880
It was the National Cathedral which offered him a pulpit

565
00:33:14,980 --> 00:33:21,320
from which to preach his message of peace to Washington in 1968.

566
00:33:21,420 --> 00:33:25,220
It was a very exciting time for the cathedral staff

567
00:33:25,320 --> 00:33:30,140
and for the city because many of the cathedral staff

568
00:33:30,240 --> 00:33:33,080
had gone to the South, particularly Selma,

569
00:33:33,180 --> 00:33:38,980
to march and to participate in the civil rights movement.

570
00:33:38,981 --> 00:33:42,760
It was also important because of what Martin Luther King had to say.

571
00:33:43,380 --> 00:33:49,080
And his challenge in that sermon was that doing what is morally right

572
00:33:49,081 --> 00:33:55,140
is more important than proper politic as well as seeking to be safe.

573
00:33:55,720 --> 00:33:58,700
And it was something that resounded around the nation.

574
00:33:58,920 --> 00:34:04,800
It was a part of what was picked up as his challenge for America to be called to its moral conscience.

575
00:34:04,801 --> 00:34:09,340
And we were very pleased that he shared his last Sunday sermon

576
00:34:09,440 --> 00:34:11,700
here in this cathedral.

577
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:14,240
But the National Cathedral is more than a forum

578
00:34:14,340 --> 00:34:16,540
to promote a moral imperative.

579
00:34:16,640 --> 00:34:21,860
It is also a facility for personal growth and reaffirmation.

580
00:34:21,960 --> 00:34:25,280
One of the traditions of great cathedrals

581
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:28,760
is to advance and support education.

582
00:34:28,860 --> 00:34:31,800
And so you will find in many of the great cathedrals,

583
00:34:31,801 --> 00:34:35,800
especially in Europe, that there are schools that are attached.

584
00:34:35,801 --> 00:34:40,800
We have here in this cathedral, as a part of its foundation,

585
00:34:40,801 --> 00:34:41,800
three schools.

586
00:34:41,801 --> 00:34:45,800
The schools have about 1,500 students in them.

587
00:34:45,801 --> 00:34:48,800
But a cathedral is also about the future.

588
00:34:48,801 --> 00:34:53,800
It is about a vision of ministry that is continual.

589
00:34:53,801 --> 00:34:57,800
We envision the continuation of our ministry

590
00:34:57,801 --> 00:34:59,800
to the city and to the world.

591
00:34:59,801 --> 00:35:03,700
We are very committed to continue our outreach in areas

592
00:35:03,800 --> 00:35:07,100
such as literacy, lobbying for social justice,

593
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:10,440
the feeding of the hungry, housing,

594
00:35:10,540 --> 00:35:14,240
things of this sort are a great concern for us.

595
00:35:14,340 --> 00:35:16,860
Perhaps it's not surprising that when people of goodwill

596
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:20,060
gather together, their efforts speak to the better angels

597
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:21,660
of our nature.

598
00:35:21,760 --> 00:35:23,660
Under the pinnacles of a Gothic cathedral,

599
00:35:23,760 --> 00:35:26,560
that dynamic seems to suggest the intimate connection

600
00:35:26,561 --> 00:35:29,060
between people and God.

601
00:35:31,360 --> 00:35:34,560
I like to think of the cathedral not so much as a convergence

602
00:35:34,660 --> 00:35:40,800
of heaven and earth, but I like to see it as the marriage

603
00:35:40,900 --> 00:35:46,520
of the human and the divine, if indeed we see the divine

604
00:35:46,620 --> 00:35:49,220
in humanity as creativity.

605
00:35:49,320 --> 00:35:52,320
And so therefore, in that sense, when I look at this cathedral

606
00:35:52,420 --> 00:35:54,620
and I look at its great pinnacles

607
00:35:54,621 --> 00:35:56,620
and I look at the carvings within it,

608
00:35:56,621 --> 00:36:00,620
and I have this sense, what faith inspired the craftsmen?

609
00:36:00,621 --> 00:36:07,620
What faith inspired the window painters and the masons?

610
00:36:07,621 --> 00:36:11,620
What inspired them to do such marvelous work?

611
00:36:11,621 --> 00:36:15,620
And I think that it has within me the kind of excitement

612
00:36:15,621 --> 00:36:20,620
that says it is inspired of God that we could create something

613
00:36:20,621 --> 00:36:23,620
that would be totally for the glory of God.

614
00:36:23,621 --> 00:36:34,380
So to me, it is the coming together of the divine in humankind in such a way that it produces a magnificent expression of our love to God.

615
00:36:40,480 --> 00:36:46,180
It was such a wonderful experience to be a part of the building of the cathedral and to create something worthy,

616
00:36:46,820 --> 00:36:49,820
worthy of the Lord and worthy of the best that this nation could offer.

617
00:36:49,821 --> 00:36:52,760
When you finish setting that last finial up there,

618
00:36:52,860 --> 00:36:55,920
I also thought of the hundreds of men I'd worked with,

619
00:36:56,020 --> 00:36:59,560
the architects who were gone, no longer here.

620
00:36:59,660 --> 00:37:03,060
I would have to say it was exhilaration, of course.

621
00:37:03,160 --> 00:37:05,160
It had to be exhilarating to do that,

622
00:37:05,260 --> 00:37:09,680
and to have 17,000 people down there watching it be set

623
00:37:09,780 --> 00:37:14,920
and to realize, but it was also a time of sadness.

624
00:37:15,020 --> 00:37:18,920
The last stone was the 1,008-pound finial,

625
00:37:18,921 --> 00:37:21,620
which was to top the west tower.

626
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:24,560
Thousands watched as a ceremonial evergreen branch

627
00:37:24,660 --> 00:37:27,660
was attached to the carving, and then it was hoisted

628
00:37:27,760 --> 00:37:29,460
over 30 stories high.

629
00:37:29,560 --> 00:37:31,360
OK, Harris, put it in third.

630
00:37:42,980 --> 00:37:46,980
The day that the cathedral was completed, I was here.

631
00:37:46,981 --> 00:37:52,880
There was almost a hush in the crowd when the stone was put in place because it truly

632
00:37:52,881 --> 00:37:59,460
was a part of man's creation and yet there was a quality about it that had a certain

633
00:37:59,461 --> 00:38:06,440
mystery to it because here was the completion of a dream of thousands of people, not only

634
00:38:06,441 --> 00:38:11,820
people who had come to visit but those artisans who had been very much a part of it.

635
00:38:11,821 --> 00:38:14,480
It brought a lump to your throat is all I can tell you.

636
00:38:14,481 --> 00:38:20,780
It was a very, very special moment indeed, and one which I feel very privileged to have been a part of.

637
00:38:21,420 --> 00:38:27,100
Otto, since 1951, you have helped set masons, set thousands of stones.

638
00:38:27,220 --> 00:38:30,240
I'm going to ask you to put the first trowel of mortar on here.

639
00:38:33,820 --> 00:38:37,400
Bring it down. We're almost over the dowel. Good.

640
00:38:37,401 --> 00:38:42,580
It actually took 83 years to finish. We started in 1907,

641
00:38:42,581 --> 00:38:47,580
And the final stone was set September 29, 1990,

642
00:38:47,581 --> 00:38:49,580
high noon on the West Tower.

643
00:38:49,581 --> 00:38:51,580
He's about got it.

644
00:38:51,581 --> 00:38:52,580
Bullseye.

645
00:38:52,581 --> 00:38:54,580
Bullseye, as he said.

646
00:38:59,580 --> 00:39:01,580
It was a very, very special day.

647
00:39:01,581 --> 00:39:03,580
It was the cathedral's birthday,

648
00:39:03,581 --> 00:39:05,580
which made it all the more special.

649
00:39:05,581 --> 00:39:09,580
And it was probably the greatest party this cathedral has ever had.

650
00:39:09,581 --> 00:39:11,080
has ever had.

651
00:39:11,081 --> 00:39:13,660
With God's help and guidance, the fabric

652
00:39:13,661 --> 00:39:16,780
of this cathedral church of St. Peter and St. Paul

653
00:39:16,781 --> 00:39:17,820
is completed.

654
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:28,140
Some buildings have a beginning, a middle, and an end.

655
00:39:28,141 --> 00:39:31,360
But a cathedral has a life which extends long after the final

656
00:39:31,361 --> 00:39:33,560
stone is set in place.

657
00:39:33,561 --> 00:39:37,260
At the National Cathedral, this ceremony was a commencement

658
00:39:37,261 --> 00:39:42,300
and an opportunity to reflect on how this Gothic structure has influenced the 20th century.

659
00:39:47,220 --> 00:39:52,740
It's a place where the history of the cathedral and of the country have been interwoven.

660
00:39:53,900 --> 00:39:56,040
When we need to grieve, we come here.

661
00:39:56,440 --> 00:39:59,720
When we want to understand, we come here.

662
00:40:00,720 --> 00:40:03,880
When we want to celebrate, we come here.

663
00:40:03,881 --> 00:40:07,480
and bells peal out on the national holidays.

664
00:40:08,240 --> 00:40:10,900
I would love to see the entire country

665
00:40:10,901 --> 00:40:15,160
discover this cathedral as America's resource, refuge,

666
00:40:15,240 --> 00:40:20,360
and reminder somewhere to strengthen the nation's heart.

667
00:40:21,980 --> 00:40:26,320
We should consecrate this place in the words of Isaiah,

668
00:40:26,820 --> 00:40:30,020
for mine house should be called a house of prayer

669
00:40:30,021 --> 00:40:34,120
for all people, all people, all America.

670
00:40:35,120 --> 00:40:38,240
God bless all of you, this magnificent cathedral,

671
00:40:38,760 --> 00:40:40,280
and the United States of America.

672
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:41,580
Thank you all very much.

673
00:40:41,581 --> 00:40:42,580
Thank you.

674
00:40:58,360 --> 00:41:01,860
Humankind's most enduring work could never match

675
00:41:01,861 --> 00:41:05,080
the infinite grace and resolve of the holy magnificence

676
00:41:05,081 --> 00:41:07,380
a cathedral celebrates.

677
00:41:07,381 --> 00:41:09,840
20 years ago, long before the National Cathedral

678
00:41:09,841 --> 00:41:13,480
was completed, restoration efforts began.

679
00:41:13,580 --> 00:41:18,140
Well, the cathedral was completed in 1990,

680
00:41:18,240 --> 00:41:20,480
but of course it was begun in 1907.

681
00:41:20,580 --> 00:41:22,080
That's 83 years of construction.

682
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:25,180
So what we've got on our hands now

683
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:28,500
is a brand new building on the west end

684
00:41:28,600 --> 00:41:32,700
and an 86-year-old building here on the east end.

685
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:35,840
And there's an awful lot going on in between.

686
00:41:35,940 --> 00:41:39,840
So in 1982, we started the first preservation work

687
00:41:39,841 --> 00:41:46,320
Well, what you're looking at on top of that finial is a type of algae that grows on limestone.

688
00:41:48,080 --> 00:41:54,320
And that's a naturally occurring phenomenon as these algaes and lichens grow on limestone and

689
00:41:54,321 --> 00:41:58,480
actually feed on the stone, the salts and the nutrients in the limestone.

690
00:41:59,200 --> 00:42:05,280
People think that stone is forever, but it wears and it weathers. When I came here,

691
00:42:05,281 --> 00:42:08,080
I thought that lead roofs were good for a couple hundred years.

692
00:42:08,880 --> 00:42:11,680
Well, they were until the internal combustion engine.

693
00:42:11,681 --> 00:42:15,520
And it's this traffic on Wisconsin Avenue out here right now and their pollution

694
00:42:15,521 --> 00:42:19,680
that's eating this cathedral alive. It's just eating it.

695
00:42:20,800 --> 00:42:25,520
An example of the way limestone begins to deteriorate is right here.

696
00:42:25,521 --> 00:42:29,920
What you're looking at are fossil fragments in the limestone.

697
00:42:29,921 --> 00:42:33,760
Of course, limestone is made up of shell particles and different fossils

698
00:42:33,761 --> 00:42:35,620
that have compacted over the years.

699
00:42:35,720 --> 00:42:38,940
And what you see here is a large fossil fragment.

700
00:42:39,040 --> 00:42:44,140
And what happens is the stone around the fossil fragments

701
00:42:44,240 --> 00:42:46,500
deteriorates, but the fossil fragment,

702
00:42:46,600 --> 00:42:49,180
being a harder material, will stay

703
00:42:49,280 --> 00:42:50,840
at the original level of the stone.

704
00:42:50,940 --> 00:42:54,140
So we use that as a gauge to help measure the deterioration

705
00:42:54,240 --> 00:42:56,120
of the limestone.

706
00:42:56,220 --> 00:42:58,080
Like Faith itself, a cathedral

707
00:42:58,180 --> 00:43:00,320
requires care and nourishment.

708
00:43:00,420 --> 00:43:03,320
Its maintenance is an act of constant rededication

709
00:43:03,321 --> 00:43:05,720
a special kind of prayer which is spoken

710
00:43:05,820 --> 00:43:08,260
through the workers' hands.

711
00:43:08,360 --> 00:43:10,660
What the masons are doing now on that scaffold

712
00:43:10,760 --> 00:43:14,160
is very carefully cutting out any deteriorated mortar

713
00:43:14,260 --> 00:43:17,300
that they see between the stones and replacing it

714
00:43:17,400 --> 00:43:21,180
with new mortar, tucking it into the joint by hand,

715
00:43:21,280 --> 00:43:23,580
the way it's been done for centuries.

716
00:43:23,680 --> 00:43:24,980
Every stone tells a story.

717
00:43:25,080 --> 00:43:28,980
It's interesting to see and think about what kind of a man,

718
00:43:29,080 --> 00:43:30,640
what kind of a mason set these stones

719
00:43:30,740 --> 00:43:33,320
that we are now working on.

720
00:43:33,321 --> 00:43:36,360
Although the investments in time and spirit were substantial,

721
00:43:36,460 --> 00:43:39,520
the financial costs were far less than the great scale

722
00:43:39,620 --> 00:43:42,460
of the project might suggest.

723
00:43:42,560 --> 00:43:46,260
The total of this cathedral is around 57, 58 million,

724
00:43:46,360 --> 00:43:48,840
which is really pretty small compared to the Hoover

725
00:43:48,940 --> 00:43:52,080
building downtown or to the price of a bomber today.

726
00:43:52,180 --> 00:43:53,880
There's the old fiction started by writers

727
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:56,380
that cathedrals are never finished.

728
00:43:56,480 --> 00:43:58,920
Well, I contend that they weren't finished

729
00:43:59,020 --> 00:44:02,440
because those who were leading it either lost the spirit,

730
00:44:02,441 --> 00:44:05,080
lost the leadership, and didn't have the support,

731
00:44:05,180 --> 00:44:06,480
or just didn't care.

732
00:44:06,580 --> 00:44:09,480
We finished ours, and we hope from that beauty,

733
00:44:09,580 --> 00:44:14,180
faith has increased in the many people who come here.

734
00:44:14,280 --> 00:44:16,360
900 years ago, the Gothic ideal

735
00:44:16,460 --> 00:44:18,620
illuminated the spiritual revelation that

736
00:44:18,720 --> 00:44:21,420
captured the imagination and offered an interpretation

737
00:44:21,520 --> 00:44:25,100
of faith which has endured for generations.

738
00:44:25,200 --> 00:44:28,260
It is not the cathedral which engenders faith,

739
00:44:28,360 --> 00:44:30,940
but as an articulation of spirit, it

740
00:44:30,941 --> 00:44:33,980
It is a timeless reminder of the glory of God,

741
00:44:34,620 --> 00:44:37,780
a glory all creatures carry in their soul.

742
00:44:38,580 --> 00:44:43,200
It is holy because we expect to meet God here.

743
00:44:43,201 --> 00:44:46,660
It is holy because it calls forth from us

744
00:44:46,661 --> 00:44:49,600
true adoration of the Holy One.

745
00:44:50,180 --> 00:44:52,100
Here we make the connection

746
00:44:52,101 --> 00:44:56,520
between the place of holy assembly and the world.

747
00:44:56,521 --> 00:45:02,820
It is meet and right to find God's presence in this place.

748
00:45:03,840 --> 00:45:07,180
He is here not in the stones.

749
00:45:08,260 --> 00:45:10,900
He is here in the people of God.

750
00:45:11,340 --> 00:45:16,720
His presence encourages us, sustains us in the world.

751
00:45:55,300 --> 00:45:56,500
CHOIR SINGS

752
00:46:25,300 --> 00:46:26,500
Oh, God.

753
00:46:26,520 --> 00:46:40,520
Alleluia.

